00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a deli penguins near the ross sea  are frequently on the move waddling and  sliding along icy surfaces  not to mention breeding or hunting for  food  their antarctic home can experience  whiteout conditions from snow  and freezing rain and winds upwards of  90 to 100 miles per hour  this leaves drones and helicopters tight  flight windows  to observe penguin colonies from above  new research published in science  robotics  took advantage of this challenge to test  an automated technique  for piloting drones in extreme  environments  its flight path algorithm was shown to  speed up penguin surveys  more than tenfold the algorithm  affectionately dubbed popcorn uses a  mathematical system  to navigate tricky environmental  barriers in ways that human piloted  drones haven't  rather than the zigzagging lawnmower  pattern of individually flown drones  popcorn takes a contoured path over a  targeted area  and layers the paths of multiple drones  this helps preserve battery life  while maintaining survey coverage  the study scientists aim their sites at  cape crosier  one of the largest adelie penguin  colonies in the world  with more than 300 000 nesting pairs  the algorithm set its course by  designing a grid of the target area  and assigning gps coordinates to points  on the grid  satellite elevation data prevented  drones from colliding with each other  in the varying slopes of cape crosier  this data also helped meet strict  regulations on airspace over penguin  colonies  and ensure the safety of the cape's  tuxedo residents  and popcorn fixed the path start and  stop positions in the same place  to simplify the search for a safe  landing site  all of this the researchers claim made  the aerial survey as  easy as pressing a button previously  human piloted drone surveys of this  region took over two  days to complete the new automated drone  network  accomplished the task in three hours  ecologists hope this technique will  provide better estimates of penguin  populations than  individual drones and satellite imagery  and  for the first time allow large aerial  surveys of their chicks  which are currently counted on the  ground beak by beak  adelie penguins also indicate changes in  climate  their hunting movements and breeding  success are smoking guns  for sea ice transformations in raw sea  the research team has already deployed  its technology  to help track bird movements in  california and livestock  on ranches one day  the team envisions popcorn contributing  to wildfire surveys  its swift operations detecting  vulnerable areas of forest  and brush under intense time pressure  as climate change and conservation force  us to rethink the way we see the world  this eye in the sky could prove a handy  and hands-free  [Music]  tool

